# bootstrap-compact.conf — box-level switch for the WEEKLY compaction run.
#
# Install to $OC_CONFIG/bootstrap-compact.conf (Mac and Contabo container:
# ~/.openclaw/, Hostinger VPS container: /data/.openclaw/). One word, nothing
# else:
#
#   report    DEFAULT. The weekly job measures every bootstrap file against its
#             lean target, prints the class and the reason for every section, and
#             prints the plan it WOULD carry out. It writes nothing at all.
#             This is what a box gets on its first scheduled week, so the first
#             thing an operator sees is a reviewable plan, not a surprise edit.
#
#   apply     The weekly job performs the plan: cold, archive and dedupe-class
#             OWNER-AUTHORED sections move to the reference root verbatim, a
#             four-line pointer is left behind, the ledger is appended, and every
#             pointer and stored block is re-verified afterwards. Hot sections and
#             script-owned blocks are still never touched — an over-target file
#             that has run out of movable content gets a proposal, not an edit.
#
# Resolution order (first match wins):
#   1. $OPENCLAW_BOOTSTRAP_COMPACT_MODE
#   2. this file
#   3. agents.defaults.bootstrapCompactMode in openclaw.json
#   4. report
#
# An unrecognised value is treated as "report" and warns. Honouring a typo as
# "apply" would write to a bootstrap file nobody authorised, which is exactly the
# failure this switch exists to prevent.
#
# Related: config/bootstrap-pointer.conf.example is the switch for the OTHER half
# of lean bootstrap — how the fleet roll stamps its own MANAGED blocks. The two
# are independent: this one governs owner-authored content moved on a schedule,
# that one governs script-owned content stamped during a roll.
report
